{
  "$comment": [
    "Every book, its USFM code, its canonical display name and every spelling accepted for",
    "it. One declaration read by both parsers, so `Mark 1:1-8` and `MRK 1:1-8` resolve the",
    "same way everywhere (#218). Matching is case-insensitive and ignores spaces and dots.",
    "`ambiguous` lists abbreviations that name more than one book; they are refused rather",
    "than resolved, because choosing one silently is how the wrong text gets read.",
    "`other_codes` are USFM codes the shipped versification schemes use that the 66-book table does not name — deuterocanonical books and their variants. They resolve to themselves so the mapper can read every scheme; no display name is invented for them.",
    "`testament` and `original_language` are declared per book, not derived from the number. They were `int(number) >= 40` and a matching guess, which is a convention nothing stated and nothing could correct. Note the simplification now visible: Ezra and Daniel have Aramaic portions, and are declared Hebrew here."
  ],
